> While I agree with other people in this thread that giving the amulet
> to Izchak is meaningless, I don't see the problem with an alternate
> ending that doesn't include the planes. As long as it isn't called an
> ascension on the score table, then it will only carry as much kudos as
> its difficulty deserves. If you want to use some easier ending then you
> should be able to do so, you just won't get the same bragging rights as
> the other guy who went up to astral. This could be reflected by the
> in-game fate of your character.
> 
> These would be alternatives to ascension, not replacements. Ascension
> would still be the most challenging (and therefore most glorious)
> possible goal in nethack, these would simply be lesser alternatives for
> people who either don't think they are equipped for a full ascension
> this time, or who find the endgame boring, or have some roleplaying
> reason to use an alternate ending, or maybe just fancy a change.
> 
> Here are some suggestions for aternative endings:
> 
> - Defy the Gods: You exit the dungeon with the amulet and go back to
> the land of your birth to study it and unlock its secrets. Once you
> have done so you wield massive power for the remainder of your mortal
> lifetime, reshaping the world according to your own designs before
> ultimately passing forever into legend. This would be a good ending for
> atheist players. It skips the plains, so there would be less kudos than
> ascension, but getting the amulet and taking it up to Dlvl1 is no mean
> feat, so it would definitely be worth a certain amount of glory.
> 
> - Convert and serve Moloch: After all, you can convert within your
> pantheon, why not convert out of it? Moloch obviously doesn't want the
> amulet sacrificed to him (or he'd have had the priest do it) but that
> doesn't mean he can't use you: Last time I brought up this subject, I
> think someone suggested that Moloch might like an altar of his own up
> on astral. Therefore, once you have the amulet, you can use it to get
> to astral and then somehow convert an altar up there to moloch (#invoke
> the amulet at a high altar? Destroy it?). This is almost as much work
> as a proper ascension (it just means you don't have to worry about
> finding the right altar, although a HoOA can do almost the same job) so
> you should get almost as much kudos: Moloch grants you immortality for
> your favour, but the other gods lay some sort of hideous eternal
> punishment upon you as well. This would be a good finish or players who
> feel they've had such a bum deal from their own pantheon throughout the
> game that they just don't deserve the amulet.

The punishment would be that you'd be guarding that amulet as the high 
priest of Moloch at the lowest level of the Gehennom. Surely this is 
where Moloch wants the amulet to be.

